/-! # Sections of powers of a point ideal on a projective curve

Source: Gieseker, Lectures on Moduli of Curves, Proposition 1.0.3, printed pp.33--34,
observations (i)--(iv), with the explicit chart and jet argument recorded in the DAG.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-point-power-section-bound)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A fixed positive bound absorbs the constant term in the linear loss of sections
imposed by powers of a reduced closed point on a one-dimensional component. The ideal
is the kernel of the canonical residue-field point, and the twist is the actual tensor
power of the restriction of O(1). Scalars on module cohomology are restricted along the
structure map induced by the embedding. Nilpotents, embedded points, disconnectedness,
and singularities at P are allowed.

Source: Gieseker, Lectures on Moduli of Curves, Proposition 1.0.3, printed pp.33--34,
observations (i)--(iv); explicit chart/jet proof in the DAG, with Stacks Tags 0AG7,
00DV, 01IN, 01IS.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-point-power-section-bound)

Proof (the six DAG steps, for stage 3):
1. Use lem-sfb-projective-closed-subscheme-homogeneous-ideal to write C as the Proj
   of its actual homogeneous coordinate quotient. The vanishing polynomials in each
   degree already form a subspace, so the span in
   def-sfb-projective-coordinate-hilbert-polynomial gives the same quotient. Apply
   lem-sfb-coordinate-quotient-section-polynomial-tail and the transition rule in
   def-pc-projective-space-twist. Choose m0 >= 1 so that for every m >= m0 the
   sections H_m of L^m have dimension delta*m+c. This bound is independent of t.
2. Choose a standard chart containing P using lem-weil-pbc-projective-space-standard-charts.
   Its intersection with C is Spec A for a quotient of k[u_j]. By
   lem-weil-pbc-closed-point-residue-field-base its closed point has residue field k.
   Write y_j=u_j-u_j(P); these generate the point maximal ideal n. Localize to
   R=A_n with maximal ideal m. The stalk of the point ideal power is m^t
   (def-sbc-closed-immersion-ideal-sheaf, def-sbc-ideal-sheaf-powers).
3. For 1 <= t <= m, each class of R/m^t is represented by a polynomial in y_j
   of degree less than t: invert s=b+h by the finite geometric series
   b^(-1)*sum(q=0,...,t-1)(-h/b)^q, then discard terms of degree at least t.
   Homogenize y^alpha to x_i^(m-|alpha|)*product(x_j-u_j(P)*x_i)^alpha_j.
   The resulting global sections show the evaluation H_m -> R/m^t is surjective,
   and the same finite monomial family proves finite-dimensionality of R/m^t.
4. By lem-sc-scheme-sober a one-dimensional component through P has a distinct
   generic point, yielding a prime q strictly below n. Choose a in n minus q.
   No power a^j/1 vanishes in R, since clearing a denominator contradicts primality.
   Thus m^j is nonzero for all j. Each is finitely generated by products of the y_j.
   Nakayama (lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-jacobson) rules out m^j=m^(j+1). The t nonzero
   successive factors in the filtration of R/m^t each have dimension at least one,
   so dim(R/m^t) >= t.
5. Trivialize L^m locally. At P, membership in the point ideal power is exactly
   membership of the germ in m^t; away from P the ideal is the whole structure sheaf.
   Local lifts of a kernel section glue uniquely. Hence the kernel of evaluation is
   H^0(C,I_P^t tensor L^m). Surjectivity and the dimensions in steps 1 and 4 give
   dim H^0(C,I_P^t tensor L^m) <= delta*m-t+c for 1 <= t <= m.
6. Choose K >= 1 with K >= c. For t=0 the ideal power is O_C and step 1 gives
   the same estimate. Both constants precede the quantifiers on m and t; no
   reducedness, connectedness, smoothness or nodality was used. -/
theorem exists_projective_point_power_section_bound
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] (n : ℕ) (C : Scheme.{u})
    (i : C ⟶ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1)); Spec (.of k))) [IsClosedImmersion i]
    (hC : topologicalKrullDim C ≤ 1) (P : C) (hP : IsClosed ({P} : Set C))
    (hcomp : ∃ Z : Set C, Z ∈ irreducibleComponents C ∧ P ∈ Z ∧
      topologicalKrullDim Z = 1)
    (δ c : ℚ) (hδ : 0 < δ)
    (hpoly : ProjectiveHilbertPolynomial.IsCoordinatePolynomial k n i
      (Polynomial.C δ * Polynomial.X + Polynomial.C c)) :
    ∃ m₀ K : ℕ, 1 ≤ m₀ ∧ 1 ≤ K ∧
      ∀ m : ℕ, m₀ ≤ m → ∀ t : ℕ, t ≤ m →
        (Module.finrank k
          ((ModuleCat.restrictScalars
            ((Scheme.ΓSpecIso (.of k)).inv ≫
              (i ≫ (ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1)); Spec (.of k)) ↘ _)).appTop).hom).obj
            ((RingedSpace.moduleCohomology C.toRingedSpace 0).obj
              ((((C.fromSpecResidueField P).ker ^ t).toModules ⊗
                ((Scheme.Modules.tensorPullback i).obj
                  (ProjectiveSpace.twist (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1)))
                    (Spec (.of k)) 1)).tensorPow m : C.Modules)))) : ℚ) ≤ δ * m - t + K := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
